{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "07", "section": "06", "chapter_title": "Productivity and Workflow Automation", "title": "Collaboration Norms", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting collaboration norms to shared document collaboration setup with access and comment controls, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the shared document collaboration setup with access and comment controls shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation", "cat100_supplement_collaboration_norms", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 6, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/07/05"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_7_6_collaboration_norms_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_7_6_collaboration_norms_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_7_6_collaboration_norms_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_7_6_collaboration_norms_calibration"]}, "content": "# Collaboration Norms\n\n![Collaboration Norms textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/07-06-collaboration-norms.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nGroup work fails far more often from missing norms than from missing effort. Four motivated students sharing one document will still collide if everyone edits the same paragraph at once, if the link is open to the world, if changes overwrite each other with no record, and if the only deadline is the real one at 11:59 PM. These are not personality problems; they are the predictable result of an unmanaged shared file. This section teaches the small set of norms that make a shared document safe to work in: least-access sharing, clear section ownership, suggesting mode, and an internal deadline ahead of the real one.\n\nThese norms are the difference between a group project that comes together calmly the day before and one that produces a last-minute scramble where someone's work silently disappears. They are also directly transferable to every workplace that uses shared documents.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Set least-access sharing so only the people who need to edit can edit, and everyone else can view.\n- Assign section ownership so no two people work on the same part at the same time.\n- Use suggesting mode and comments so edits are visible and reversible, never silent overwrites.\n- Set an internal deadline ahead of the real one to leave time for review.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA four-person team shares one document set to \"anyone with the link can edit.\" All four open the introduction at once on the night it is due. Two of them type over each other; the document keeps only the last save, so one person's paragraph vanishes with no trace. The final section is pasted in at 11:55 PM for an 11:59 PM deadline, leaving zero time to read the whole thing for coherence. Nothing here was caused by laziness; it was caused by the absence of four simple rules.\n\nThe fix is to agree on access, ownership, edit mode, and timing before anyone starts writing, so the document structure itself prevents the collisions.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nCollaboration norms manage four risks that shared documents create.\n\n**Access risk** is who can change the file. The principle is least access: grant edit rights only to the actual contributors and leave everyone else on view-only. \"Anyone with the link can edit\" invites accidental or malicious changes from outside the group.\n\n**Collision risk** is two people editing the same content at once. The fix is **section ownership**: each contributor owns a distinct part (intro, methods, results, conclusion), so simultaneous edits never land on the same text. Ownership turns a free-for-all into parallel, non-overlapping work.\n\n**Overwrite risk** is one edit silently erasing another. **Suggesting mode** (proposed changes the owner accepts or rejects) plus comments makes every change visible and reversible, so nothing disappears without a record. This is the collaborative analogue of the version history you would keep for your own files.\n\n**Timing risk** is having no margin to review. An **internal deadline** set 24 hours before the real one reserves time for the team to read the assembled whole, catch contradictions between sections, and fix them before submission.\n\nThe unifying idea is that good collaboration is designed, not hoped for: you set up the document so the common failures cannot happen, rather than asking everyone to be careful.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A team says \u00e2\u20ac\u2122we all trust each other, so we can just share one open document and each edit wherever we want.\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 Which risk does that trust leave completely unmanaged?\" explanation=\"Trust addresses bad intent, but the failures in shared documents are honest collisions: two trusted teammates open the same paragraph and one save erases the other. Section ownership and suggesting mode, not trust, prevent that.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Collision and overwrite risk, since trusted teammates still type over the same paragraph\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"None; mutual trust removes every collaboration risk\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Only access risk, which trust fully resolves\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Only timing risk, which trust makes irrelevant\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is a real group document moved from chaos to working norms.\n\n**Before (one shared file, no rules):**\n\n```\nSharing:  Anyone with link can EDIT\nOwners:   none; all four edit the intro at once\nTiming:   last section pasted at 11:55 PM for an 11:59 PM due time\nEdits:    teammates overwrite each other's text with no record\n```\n\nA stranger could edit it, two people collided on the intro, and there was no time to review.\n\n**After (named access, owners, suggestions, internal deadline):**\n\n```\nSharing:  4 named teammates as EDITORS; everyone else VIEW only\nOwners:   Intro = Ana, Methods = Ben, Results = Cy, Conclusion = Dee\nTiming:   internal deadline Thursday 11:59 PM (24h before the real one)\nEdits:    use Suggesting mode + comments so changes are visible and reversible\n```\n\nFour norms did the work:\n\n1. **Least-access sharing**: named editors only, not public edit.\n2. **Section owners** so no two people retype the same paragraph.\n3. **Suggesting mode and comments** so edits never silently overwrite a teammate.\n4. **An internal deadline 24 hours early** so the team can review before submitting.\n\nSet up the shared file one decision at a time, predicting each norm before it is revealed.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Configure a four-person shared document before anyone writes\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The document is currently set to \u00e2\u20ac\u2122anyone with the link can edit.\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 First decision: access. What do you change it to?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Leave it open so nobody is locked out\",\"Add the four named teammates as editors and set everyone else to view-only\",\"Make it editable only by the group leader\",\"Set it to view-only for all four so nothing breaks\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Least access. Named editors for the four contributors and view-only for everyone else keeps outsiders from changing the file without locking the team out.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Access is set. Next, all four still plan to write the intro together. How do you remove the collision risk?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Tell everyone to be careful in the intro\",\"Assign one owner per section: Intro = Ana, Methods = Ben, Results = Cy, Conclusion = Dee\",\"Have everyone write in a personal copy and merge later by hand\",\"Lock the document so only one person edits at a time\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Section ownership. One owner per part means simultaneous edits never land on the same text, turning a free-for-all into parallel work.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Owners are assigned. How should teammates make changes to each other\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s sections so nothing is silently erased?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Edit directly and tell the owner afterward\",\"Use suggesting mode and comments so each change is visible and reversible\",\"Only the owner may ever touch a section\",\"Track changes in a separate text file\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Visibility. Suggesting mode plus comments makes every change something the owner accepts or rejects, so no edit overwrites another without a record.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Last, set an internal deadline 24 hours before the real one so the team can read the assembled whole, catch contradictions between sections, and fix them before submission. Access, ownership, visibility, timing: the document is now designed so the common failures cannot happen.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore a group starts working in a shared file, decide four things: who can edit (least access), who owns which section, how edits will be made visible (suggesting mode), and when the internal deadline falls. Settle these before the writing, not during the crisis.\n\nA well-run shared document passes four checks:\n\n1. **Access check:** Can only the actual contributors edit, with everyone else on view-only?\n2. **Ownership check:** Does each section have one clear owner, so no two people edit the same part?\n3. **Visibility check:** Are changes made through suggesting mode and comments, so nothing is silently overwritten?\n4. **Timing check:** Is there an internal deadline ahead of the real one to leave room for review?\n\nIf any check fails, the document is set up to produce the exact collisions group projects are famous for.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misreading is \"we trust each other, so we do not need rules.\" Trust does not prevent two people from opening the same paragraph at once; ownership and edit mode do. The norms protect good teammates from honest collisions, not just from bad actors.\n\nA second misreading is treating the assignment deadline as the team deadline. With no internal deadline, the document is finished at the moment it is due, with no time to read it as a whole.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the norms to a slide deck instead of a written report, where the same risks appear in a different shape. Access: invite the four presenters as editors, set the link to view-only for the rest of the class who will see it later. Ownership: assign slides by topic so two people never restyle the same slide and overwrite each other's layout.\n\nVisibility is trickier in slides, where suggesting mode may not exist, so the norm adapts to \"edit your own slides only, and leave a comment before changing someone else's.\" Timing: set the internal deadline to the night before the presentation so the team can do a full run-through and fix the slide where two sections repeat the same statistic. The walkthrough shows the four norms holding across document types, with the visibility norm adapting to the tool's features.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader looks at a shared document and can predict where it will break before any work happens. An open edit link, no section owners, and only the real deadline are red flags regardless of how good the team is.\n\nA strong reader of a collaborative document can answer four questions:\n\n1. Who can edit this, and is anyone with access who should not have it?\n2. Who owns each section, and where could two people collide on the same content?\n3. If a teammate's text were overwritten tonight, could it be recovered, and would anyone notice?\n4. Is there time reserved to read the assembled whole before it is due?\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_collaboration_norms\", \"cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is the document's collaboration setup: its sharing settings, an ownership map, the edit mode, and the internal deadline. It is strong when a reader can see that the four risks are each addressed before any content is written.\n\nA useful note sounds like this: \"I changed the sharing from public-edit to four named editors and assigned each of us a section, then set the document to suggesting mode. I rejected keeping a single shared intro that everyone edits, because that is exactly where our last project lost a paragraph to an overwrite.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the norms from a class group project to a workplace shared drive. Least access, ownership, visibility, and an internal deadline all transfer, but access control becomes stricter and more consequential: at work, the wrong person editing a client file can have legal or financial consequences, so view/edit permissions are governed by role and policy, not just convenience. The four norms hold; what adapts is that access decisions move from a casual group choice to a policy-bound one.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the collaboration norms packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/07-06-collaboration-norms-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"shared document collaboration setup with access, ownership, and deadlines\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"07.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the shared document collaboration setup.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the access-and-ownership rule to a new setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the setup.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the shared document collaboration setup with access and comment controls for CAT 100 07.06. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_collaboration_norms\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm your shared document passes the access, ownership, visibility, and timing checks: least-access sharing, one owner per section, suggesting mode for changes, and an internal deadline ahead of the real one.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_7_6_collaboration_norms_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_collaboration_norms", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_collaboration_norms", "cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation"], "stem": "Your four-person group needs to edit one shared report. Which sharing setting fits the task?", "options": ["Anyone with the link can edit", "Share to the four named teammates as editors; anyone else gets view-only or no access", "Make it fully public on the web", "Email a copy to each person so there are four versions"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Give the team the least access they need. Public edit invites strangers; four separate copies fragment the work.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_7_6_anyone_with_link_edit", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_6_collaboration_norms_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_collaboration_norms", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_collaboration_norms"], "stem": "You disagree with a teammate's paragraph in a shared doc. What keeps the change visible and reversible?", "options": ["Delete it and retype your version", "Use Suggesting mode or a comment so the teammate can see and accept or reject the change", "Rewrite it overnight without telling anyone", "Lock them out of the document"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Suggestions and comments preserve the original and show what changed, instead of silently overwriting a teammate.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_7_6_overwrite_teammate", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_6_collaboration_norms_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_collaboration_norms", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_collaboration_norms"], "